Found while fixing #142, one command over, and left alone there only
because that agent was scoped to Cmd/Skill.pm.

lib/App/karr/Cmd/Init.pm, in `_install_claude_skill` (the
`karr init --claude-skill` path), writes .claude/skills/karr/SKILL.md with
the call #142 just removed from Cmd/Skill.pm:

    eval { $target->spew_utf8($skill_content); 1 }
      or user_error( "Could not write $target: ", clean_error($@) );

spew_utf8 is temp+rename, so the target comes back on a new inode and the
manage-skills chain breaks exactly as it did through `karr skill update`:
that checkout gets the new text, the other links keep the old inode, link
count drops silently.

Minimal fix is the same one-liner:

    $target->append_utf8( { truncate => 1 }, $skill_content )

The fuller fix is to route it through the same helper as Cmd/Skill.pm's
`_write_skill`, so there is one place that knows how a skill file has to
be written, including the read-only fallback and its warning. Prefer that
if the two commands can share it without contorting either -- a second
copy of this rule is how the first one drifted.

Reference for the in-place contract and the test shape:
t/144-skill-write-in-place.t.
Fixed in f4f3f90 by sharing, not copying: _write_skill moved into the new App::karr::Role::SkillFile, composed by both Cmd::Skill and Cmd::Init. It fits because the helper never touched $self -- one role can serve a board-less command and a board command by depending on neither. Init keeps its own mkpath guard ahead of the shared call, since 'Could not create .claude/...' is what actually fails there (pinned by t/120). Verified both commands get the same coderef. Test t/146-init-skill-write-in-place.t; the new role was added to t/121's leakage sweep so it is actually covered.
